WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.763
Servers sit in data centres and clouds, where you have no keyboard and no screen.

2
00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:12.773
SSH gives you a shell on such a machine, over an encrypted connection.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:23.951
Running ssh ubuntu@192.168.64.5 logs you in as ubuntu, on the machine at that address.

4
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:27.595
Passwords work, but people guess them and reuse them.

5
00:00:27.595 --> 00:00:29.407
Keys are safer, and quicker.

6
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:32.932
Create a key pair with ssh-keygen.

7
00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:39.241
ed25519 is the modern key type, and the comment helps you tell keys apart.

8
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:41.015
A key pair is two files.

9
00:00:41.015 --> 00:00:43.403
The private key stays on your laptop.

10
00:00:43.403 --> 00:00:47.081
It works like a password, so never copy it anywhere else.

11
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.414
The public key is safe to share.

12
00:00:49.414 --> 00:00:51.857
You put it on servers, and in GitHub.

13
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:53.966
Set a passphrase when it asks.

14
00:00:53.966 --> 00:00:58.587
It encrypts the private key file, so a stolen copy is useless without it.

15
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:06.529
And ls -l shows the private key with mode 600, for the owner only, and the public key with 644.

16
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:12.436
The server keeps the public keys it trusts in a file called authorized_keys, one per line.

17
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:16.257
So a copy of your public key goes there, as one more line.

18
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.292
Now connect.

19
00:01:17.292 --> 00:01:19.073
Trust has to run both ways.

20
00:01:19.073 --> 00:01:25.672
First, your laptop checks that this really is your server, which has a key of its own, the host key.

21
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:33.523
The first time you connect, ssh shows the server's fingerprint, and asks if you are sure you want to continue connecting.

22
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:37.985
Say yes, and ssh stores it in known_hosts, on your laptop.

23
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.131
Next time, ssh compares the server's fingerprint with the stored one.

24
00:01:43.131 --> 00:01:45.035
They match, so it carries on.

25
00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:53.183
If that fingerprint ever changes, ssh refuses to connect, because someone may be pretending to be your server.

26
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.763
Then the server checks you.

27
00:01:55.763 --> 00:02:00.008
Your ssh takes a piece of data that is unique to this connection.

28
00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:02.651
It signs that data with the private key.

29
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:04.585
The result is a signature.

30
00:02:04.585 --> 00:02:07.360
Only the signature crosses the connection.

31
00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:12.075
The server checks that signature with your public key, from authorized_keys.

32
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.042
It matches, so you are in.

33
00:02:14.042 --> 00:02:17.391
And the private key itself never left your laptop.

34
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.685
Two things make everyday use easier.

35
00:02:20.685 --> 00:02:25.495
A Host entry in your ssh config file gives the server a short name.

36
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.312
So ssh devbox replaces the long command.

37
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:34.895
And ssh-agent holds your unlocked key, so you type the passphrase only once.

38
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.463
The private key stays home.

39
00:02:37.463 --> 00:02:40.009
The public key goes in authorized_keys.

40
00:02:40.009 --> 00:02:42.163
known_hosts remembers the server.

41
00:02:42.163 --> 00:02:44.644
And only a signature crosses the wire.
